# Application
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# Database (required). Credentials match docker-compose.yml's defaults
# (POSTGRES_USER/PASSWORD/DB all default to `chatofy`), so this works against
# `docker compose up -d postgres` with no edit. The db-backed e2e suite reads the
# same variable.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://chatofy:chatofy@localhost:5432/chatofy

# Dev and CI ONLY, and only for one command: `prisma migrate diff
# --from-migrations`, which proves the migration chain and schema.prisma still
# agree. Prisma replays the whole chain into this database to compare it, so it
# must be a SEPARATE, disposable one — the diff drops and recreates its contents.
# Never point it at a database holding anything.
#
# A deployment runs `migrate deploy`, which needs no shadow database. Leave it
# unset there: it is read only when present, because Prisma rejects an EMPTY
# value outright and that would break every other command.
#
#   docker compose exec postgres psql -U chatofy -d postgres \
#     -c 'CREATE DATABASE chatofy_shadow'
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=

# Auth (required). Signs and verifies every access token the API issues. There is
# no "auth off" mode — the app refuses to start without this. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=

# Redis (optional to set; the default matches docker-compose.yml). Holds the
# rotating refresh-token families — one hash per token and one per family, both
# SHA-256 keyed, never a plaintext credential.
#
# The API BOOTS WITHOUT IT. A stopped Redis costs new sign-ins and token
# renewals, and answers 503 rather than 401 so nobody is signed out over an
# outage. `pnpm --filter api test:e2e` does need one running: the fast e2e
# suites log in for real, and login mints a family.
#
# Still NOT used by the request throttle, whose storage remains this process's
# memory — every per-route limit is per replica. That swap is a separate change.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Google login. Every OAuth client id whose id_tokens this API will accept,
# comma-separated. Unset disables the route with a clear error rather than
# blocking the boot — web needs one id, mobile adds a per-platform id later.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=

# ── Mail (Gmail SMTP) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read only where NODE_ENV is NEITHER development NOR test. In dev and test the
# console sender always wins and prints links to stdout, whatever is set here —
# so register/verify/forgot/reset work with no Gmail account, and a stray SMTP
# password in a dev .env can never mail a live account-takeover link. To send
# real mail you need NODE_ENV set to something else AND all four values below;
# SMTP_USER wants a Gmail app password (requires 2FA on that account, regular
# passwords are rejected). In such a deployment, missing SMTP means every mail
# is dropped and logged rather than sent, and `NODE_ENV=production` refuses to
# boot at all — see apps/api/src/modules/mail/mail.module.ts and main.ts.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# Optional display-name override, e.g. "Chatofy <SMTP_USER value>". Gmail
# rewrites `From` to the authenticated SMTP_USER address regardless, so an
# address here different from SMTP_USER is silently dropped by Gmail itself.
MAIL_FROM=

# Origin every mailed link (verify, reset, account-exists notice) is built
# from. The default matches the port apps/web runs on in dev. Production MUST
# override this — the app refuses to boot in production with the default
# still set, since a plausible-looking wrong origin in a mailed link is worse
# than an obviously broken one.
WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ── Object storage (Cloudflare R2) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# One bucket for the whole project; avatars live under an `avatars/` prefix and
# a later feature adds its own. The bucket is PUBLIC-READ, and a prefix is a
# namespace rather than an access boundary — R2 publishes a bucket as a unit and
# scopes tokens per bucket, not per prefix. Anything that must stay private goes
# in a different bucket. See docs/system-architecture.md.
# OPTIONAL in development, and unset is the normal state. With any of the five
# missing, PUT /auth/me/avatar and DELETE /auth/me/avatar answer 409 with a
# message naming the cause; everything else works normally and the API boots.
#
# 409 rather than 503 deliberately: the shared error contract has no 5xx code but
# INTERNAL_ERROR, and the exception filter replaces every 5xx message — so a 503
# would be indistinguishable from a crash. A 4xx keeps its message.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is the bucket's public custom domain, NOT the
# r2.cloudflarestorage.com endpoint (that is the S3 API and is not publicly
# readable). The web app reads the SAME variable as a build arg to generate its
# CSP img-src, so in production changing the origin is a web rebuild as well as
# an api restart.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ── Turn-based translate pipeline (vi↔en) ───────────────────────────────────
# Provider selection for the REST POST /translate flow.
# Speech runs locally by default — no API key, no cloud call. Switch either to
# `elevenlabs` (and set ELEVENLABS_API_KEY) to compare against the cloud path.
AI_STT_PROVIDER=local
AI_TTS_PROVIDER=local
AI_TRANSLATION_PROVIDER=gemini

# Provider API keys.
# A Gemini key is required — translation is still cloud, so the local speech
# stack alone does not make POST /translate work offline.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY is only needed when a provider above is set to elevenlabs.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# GEMINI_API_KEY takes SEVERAL keys, comma-separated. The translator then
# rotates turns across them, so one key's free-tier ceiling no longer ends a
# long live session:
#   GEMINI_API_KEY=key-from-project-a,key-from-project-b,key-from-project-c
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING KEYS — more keys only help under one condition.
# Gemini meters quota per PROJECT per model, not per key (the rejection body
# says so: "GenerateRequestsPerMinutePerProjectPerModel-FreeTier"). Keys issued
# from the SAME Google Cloud project therefore share ONE bucket and rotation
# buys exactly nothing. Each key must come from a different project.
#
# Also note: enabling billing on a project REMOVES that project's free tier, so
# a billed project contributes nothing to a free-tier pool. If you are willing
# to attach a card, one billed project on a paid tier is simpler than several.
#
# Exact duplicates and blank entries are dropped. Same-project keys cannot be
# detected from here — that one is on you.

# Which ElevenLabs voice speaks the translation. Read only when a provider
# above is set to `elevenlabs`; leave unset to take that backend's own default
# ("Rachel"). One id is one voice of one gender, and the backend speaks in it
# whatever gender a turn asks for — it is the cloud comparison baseline, not a
# voice picker.
ELEVENLABS_TTS_VOICE_ID=

# Local speech sidecars — start them with `pnpm dev:all` (see their READMEs for
# one-time `uv sync` + model download). Each serves both languages.
#   services/local-stt  — Zipformer (vi) + Moonshine (en) speech-to-text
#   services/local-tts  — Kokoro-82M (en) + VieNeu (vi) speech synthesis
# Default voices live in the sidecars, since a voice means a speaker id for the
# English engine and a preset name for the Vietnamese one.
LOCAL_STT_URL=http://localhost:8002
LOCAL_TTS_URL=http://localhost:8003

# Master switch for per-turn speaker embeddings — the acoustic layer that names
# who spoke without anybody being asked. Off, and both halves must be on before
# a single vector is computed: this, and the client's own `embedSpeaker` on
# `client.session.start`. The client half exists because apps/api and apps/web
# do not deploy atomically, so a tab that predates the event never asks for it;
# this half exists because a client may not decide for itself whether a voice is
# turned into a vector.
#
# **Off HERE and on in production**, which is deliberate and worth knowing before
# you chase a difference between the two. Locally it costs a model load in the
# local-stt sidecar for a feature most dev work does not touch; on the deployed
# host it is on so the real browser channel gets measured, since every threshold
# behind it was calibrated on corpus audio that never passed through
# `noiseSuppression` or `autoGainControl`. It is on there to be MEASURED, not
# because the bench says it is ready — that reads 0.78 clean and 0.59 far-field
# against a 0.85 target. See docs/system-architecture.md.
#
# Set it to true if you are working on the acoustic layer; prod behaviour is what
# you will then be reproducing.
#
# Accepts true/false/1/0 only; empty falls back to off.
SPEAKER_EMBEDDING_ENABLED=false

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of this app. 0 = none (direct port).
# REQUIRED to be set correctly behind an ingress or CDN: the auth routes are
# rate limited per client IP, and with this at 0 every request looks like it
# came from the proxy, so one attacker's login attempts exhaust the limit for
# every user. A count, never `true` — `true` makes X-Forwarded-For spoofable
# and removes the limit altogether.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=0

# CORS — comma-separated origins, or * for wildcard
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# Append one JSON line per streamed turn, timed stage by stage. Unset writes
# nothing — a latency table is collected deliberately, not grown on every
# deployment. See packages/realtime-client/src/audio/pipeline-latency.measure.spec.ts.
# A relative path resolves against this app's cwd, not the repo root, so the
# harness directory is ../../benchmarks/realtime/turns.jsonl from here.
TURN_METRICS_PATH=
